{"_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": ""}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "Data Warehouse Architecture Layers "}, {"type": "position", "content": 10}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 3}, {"type": "level", "content": "B1.1"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you use to talk about data warehouse architecture. "}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Designs the data structure based on the database structures and its characteristics.  Understand the concept of data warehouse, its components and characteristics."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Discusses a range of options for potential actions at work through the correct use of grammar, vocabulary; level of formality and purpose, and cohesion and coherence aspects appropriate to this level.  Exchanges simple information on the data validation and data warehouses."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "enpe8n5le03ob10re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Would Rather for What is Preferable: Interrogative"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "You can use the WOULD RATHER to talk about something that you prefer."}, {"type": "text", "content": "<p>When you use WOULD RATHER in questions you need to keep in mind that you are giving some options to be chosen.</p> <p>To create an interrogative statement using WOULD RATHER you need to use the following</p> <p>Structure:</p> <p><strong>Would </strong>+ Pronoun + <strong>Rather</strong> + Verb + Complement + ?</p> <p>Example:<strong><i> Would</i></strong><i> you<strong> rather</strong> use a single or a second layer architecture? </i>&nbsp;</p>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Would you rather use a single, second or third layer architecture?"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Well, I don't know. Would your boss rather prefer a single, second or third layer? "}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "need "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a necessity "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: You ought to choose the layer that fulfills your needs and goals."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n5le03ob10aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "architecture "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "the structure of something"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Hi Monica, I'm changing the data warehouse architectures because the boss asked me to."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n5le03ob10aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "redundancy "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "something that is repeated"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Basically it takes away any redundancy of information."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n5le03ob10aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "weakness "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "lack of strength"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: There is a weakness and a strength; as you know, we might want to have analytical and transactional processes separated, in this case this layer doesn't comply with this requirement."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n5le03ob10aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "available "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "that is ready for use"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Look, the first layer architecture aims to minimize the amount of data available in the warehouse."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n5le03ob10aud05"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "grab "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to obtain something"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: The third layer materializes operational data grabbed after combining source data."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n5le03ob10aud06"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Look for a company that uses data warehouse layers."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Look for which layer and how the company uses it."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Analyze the pros and cons of using that layer in that company."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Analyze the pros and cons of using that layer in that company."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Present your work to the class."}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I find a company that uses data warehouse layers."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I look for the way they use the layers."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I analyze the pros and cons of the layer in that company."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I make a chart comparing my findings."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I share my work with my classmates."}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Data Warehouse Architecture Layers"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "<p>According to the needs required by a company, it is possible to use different architecture layers for <strong>data warehouse</strong>.</p>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<p>Every <strong>data warehouse</strong> has the same properties: separation, scalability, extensibility, security and administerability. However, two different classifications are made according to the architecture layers used, these are enterprise oriented or department oriented.</p> <p>The architecture layers to be used are single-layer, second-layer, and third-layer architecture. A single-layered architecture is not commonly used, yet its main purpose is to minimize the amount of information available and it aims to avoid redundancy.</p> <p>The second-layer architecture has different phases to be followed since its main characteristic is to separate physical sources and <strong>data warehouse</strong>. Its main stages are source layer, data staging, d<strong>ata warehouse</strong> layer, and analysis.</p> <p>The third-layer architecture basically materializes findings after integrating data. Its main feature is that it creates a neutral data model for the company. One of its disadvantages is that it creates redundancy talking about operational data.</p>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Look, the first layer architecture aims to minimize the amount of data available in the warehouse. Basically it takes away any redundancy of information. There is a weakness and a strength; as you know, we might want to have analytical and transactional processes separated, in this case this layer doesn't comply with this requirement. As a strength, you'd better use this layer just in case the amount of data to be analyzed is huge, of course you need to make some arrangements for that to happen.  "}, {"type": "example", "content": "The second layer aims to the separation of data warehouse. It separates physical sources from data warehouse. As its name shows, the main idea of this layer is to have different data flow phases."}], "type": "learning_points"}], "type": "sena", "labs": [{"stimuli": [{"template_override": 1, "id": 0, "_data": [], "contents": [{}], "type": "welcome"}, {"template_override": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"images": [{"time": "100", "image": "enpe8n5le03ob10re2img01.jpg", "id": 0, "text": "<p><strong>Fred</strong>: Hi, Monica. I&rsquo;m changing the data warehouse architectures because the boss asked me so. However, I don&rsquo;t know how to do it; I don&rsquo;t know what layers I ought to use.</p>\n<p><strong>Monica</strong>: You might want to choose the layer that fulfills your needs and goals. Would you rather use a single, second, or third layer architecture?</p>\n<p><strong>Fred</strong>: Well, that&rsquo;s the issue. I don&rsquo;t have a clear picture of what they are. Would you mind instructing me?</p>"}, {"time": "24000", "image": "enpe8n5le03ob10re2img02.jpg", "id": 1, "text": "<p><strong>Monica</strong>: No problem. Look, the first layer architecture aims at minimizing the amount of data available in the warehouse. Basically, it takes away any redundancy in information. There are weakness and strength. As you know, we might want to have analytical and transactional processes separated. In this case, this layer doesn&rsquo;t comply with this requirement. As a strength, you&rsquo;d better use this layer if the amount of data to be analyzed is huge; of course, you need to make some arrangements for that to happen.&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Fred</strong>: What about the second layer?</p>\n<p><strong>Monica</strong>: The second layer aims at the separation of the data warehouse. It separates physical sources from the data warehouse. As its name shows, the main idea of this layer is to have different data flow phases.</p>"}, {"time": "66500", "image": "enpe8n5le03ob10re2img03.jpg", "id": 2, "text": "<p><strong>Fred</strong>: I guess that the third layer is more complex, am I right?</p>\n<p><strong>Monica</strong>: Yes, you are. The third layer materializes operational data grabbed after combining source data. The most important characteristic of this layer is that it creates a conventional data reference for the company. Also, it separates problems when extracting and integrating data.</p>\n<p><strong>Fred</strong>: Would you rather use a second or third layer architecture?</p>\n<p><strong>Monica</strong>: Well, I don&rsquo;t know. Would your boss rather prefer a single, second or third layer?&nbsp;</p>"}], "coverimg": "enpe8n5le03ob10re2img01.jpg", "audiosrc": "enpe8n5le03ob10re2aud01"}, "type": "slideshow"}], "type": "audio", "_data": [], "